From: ElXreno <elxreno@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] dnode: do not depend on the allocator poisoning a freed dnode

dnode_move() treats a dnode as live if its objset pointer passes
POINTER_IS_VALID(), and the comment there explains why that is enough:
"the memory patterns written by kmem (baddcafe and deadbeef) set at least
one of the two low bits". That is an illumos property. The Linux
allocator writes no such pattern, so a dnode that has been handed out by
kmem_cache_alloc() but not yet filled in by dnode_create(), which sets
the objset pointer last of all precisely so that this check works, can
carry anything at all there, including a NULL that POINTER_IS_VALID()
happily accepts. The result is dnode_move() walking into mutex_enter() on
a NULL objset.

Invalidate the pointer in the constructor, so that the window between the
allocation and the end of dnode_create() is closed by the code that
depends on it rather than by what an allocator happens to leave behind.
---
diff --git a/module/zfs/dnode.c b/module/zfs/dnode.c
--- a/module/zfs/dnode.c
+++ b/module/zfs/dnode.c
@@ -152,6 +152,16 @@
 	zfs_refcount_create(&dn->dn_tx_holds);
 	list_link_init(&dn->dn_link);
 
+	/*
+	 * dnode_move() decides whether a dnode is live by looking at this
+	 * pointer, and it must not accept one that dnode_create() has not
+	 * finished setting up yet. On illumos that follows from the allocator
+	 * writing 0xdeadbeef and 0xbaddcafe over a freed buffer, which leaves
+	 * one of the two low bits set, but nothing writes those patterns here.
+	 * Say it outright instead: a dnode is not movable until it is created.
+	 */
+	POINTER_INVALIDATE(&dn->dn_objset);
+
 	memset(dn->dn_next_type, 0, sizeof (dn->dn_next_type));
 	memset(dn->dn_next_nblkptr, 0, sizeof (dn->dn_next_nblkptr));
 	memset(dn->dn_next_nlevels, 0, sizeof (dn->dn_next_nlevels));
